/** @page v8javascript_exceptionsguide JavaScript Exceptions

    @tableofcontents

    Exceptions in JavaScript are propagated to %Qore as %Qore exceptions.

    A promise that is rejected without a handler does not end the program: it is reported as a Node.js
    \c UnhandledPromiseRejectionWarning on standard error, and the program stays usable (the module initializes
    Node with \c --unhandled-rejections=warn).  A handler the program registers with
    <tt>process.on('unhandledRejection', ...)</tt> receives the rejection as usual.  An exception thrown outside any
    call - for example in a timer callback - and a call to \c process.exit() still end the program.
*/
